# Testing Methodology

When generating testing rules, incorporate these principles adapted to the specific test framework.

## Testing Philosophy

TestingPhilosophy {
  ArchitectureEnablesTesting => good separation minimizes mocking needs
  PreferBuiltIn => use framework's tools before adding dependencies
  Determinism => control all inputs (time, randomness, IO)
  IntegrationAtBoundaries => mock only at system edges, not between internal modules
}

(extensive mocking required) => architecture problem, improve separation
(adding test utility dep) => check if framework provides equivalent
(flaky test) => likely non-deterministic input, isolate and control

## The 5 Questions

Every test must answer:

FiveQuestions {
  1. "What is the unit under test?" // Named in describe block
  2. "What is the expected behavior?" // Given/should format
  3. "What is the actual output?" // Unit exercised
  4. "What is the expected output?" // Explicit value
  5. "How can we find the bug?" // Clear from above
}

## RITE Way

RITEDefinitions {
  Readable = "given/should format, self-contained, no hunting"
  Isolated = "no shared mutable state, each test owns context"
  Thorough = "expected cases + edge cases + error cases"
  Explicit = "factory functions, no magic values, no hidden setup"
}

## Test Structure Pattern

testStructure() {
  """
  describe '[Unit Under Test]':
    test '[scenario]':
      given: [context]
      should: [behavior]
      actual: [exercise unit]
      expected: [explicit value]
  """
}

FrameworkAdaptations {
  "Jest/Vitest" => describe/it or test
  "pytest" => class Test.../def test_...
  "Go" => func TestX(t *testing.T)
}

## Test Isolation

IsolationRules {
  NoSharedMutableState => use factory functions
  NoOrderDependencies => each test from known state
  NoExternalDeps => mock services, test doubles
}

isolationPattern() {
  """
  // BAD - shared
  const testUser = { name: 'Test' };

  // GOOD - factory
  const createTestUser = () => ({ name: 'Test' });
  """
}

## Assertion Guidelines

AssertionRules {
  OneLogicalAssertion => per test (multiple asserts ok if one concept)
  BehaviorNotImplementation => result.includes(x) over array.length
  SemanticAssertions => follow @aiddgen/rules/guidance/programming-principles.mdc naming
}

## Test Organization

OrganizationRules {
  Colocation => tests near code (user.js, user.test.js)
  Naming => *.test.js, *_test.go, test_*.py
}

## State Management Testing

StateTesting {
  Selectors => always use for state access, never direct
  Setup => via action creators, not manual state
  PublicAPI => actions + selectors only
}

stateTestPattern() {
  """
  // Set up via actions
  const state = [addUser({ name: 'Test' })].reduce(reducer, undefined)

  // Assert via selectors
  assert selectFirstUser(state).name === 'Test'
  """
}

## Side Effects Testing

EffectsTesting {
  Yield => test effect descriptions, not execution
  Drive => iterator.next() with mock data
}

sagaTestPattern() {
  """
  const gen = fetchUserSaga({ payload: { id: '42' } });
  assert gen.next().value === call(fetchUser, '42')
  assert gen.next(mockUser).value === put(userLoaded(mockUser))
  """
}

## Framework Tools

When generating stack-specific testing rules, prefer built-in utilities:

BuiltInPreference {
  Spies => framework's built-in spy/stub utilities
  Mocking => framework's module mock system
  Timers => framework's fake timers for time-dependent code
  Fixtures => framework's fixture system over custom setup
}

PartialMocking {
  Pattern => import real, override specific
  Rationale => preserve integration, mock only boundaries
}

## Lifecycle Calibration

| Aspect | PoC | MVP | Production |
|--------|-----|-----|------------|
| Coverage | Skip | Critical paths | Comprehensive |
| Isolation | Basic | Required | Strict |
| Speed | Not critical | Fast | Must be fast |
| Edge cases | Ignore | Likely ones | Thorough |
| 5 Questions | Optional | Encouraged | Required |
| RITE | Loose | Moderate | Strict |
